Brief summary: Idk just read the statement
Comment: Seems like there is a lot of ways to solve this problem, but I solved it using batching on queries.
Even though relaxing $block$ updates works in linear time with just 1 dfs, and query takes $block$ binary searched on childen of some vertex, the optimal $block$ turned out to be > $sqrt(n)$.
Recursive dfs is too slow to rely on it. Flattening the graf sped up things by a lot. With turning dfs into a pass on an array, optimal block shifted closer to $sqrt(n)$.
